f 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530327 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530380 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dcb4ea53-f6ce-50a3-91c6-7ce13b2fa771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530419 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0512b677-42e3-54b0-95e1-80a717c5187c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530419 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530452 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530452 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530472 #