\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530517 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530517 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solutio